\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

\paragraph{Datasets.}
We evaluate on four English NER benchmarks spanning a difficulty gradient, each serving a distinct role.
\textbf{SciERC} \cite{luan2018scierc} (551 test instances, 6 entity types) is the primary medium-difficulty test bed where models achieve ${\approx}$65\% F1.
\textbf{Few-NERD} \cite{ding2021fewnerd} (INTRA coarse; 37{,}648 instances, 8 entity types, ${\approx}$75\% F1) provides a low-degeneracy contrast where LP selection succeeds (\S\ref{sec:fewnerd}).
\textbf{CoNLL-2003} \cite{tjongkimsang2003conll} (3{,}453 test sentences, 4 entity types, ${\approx}$91\% F1) serves as a ceiling-effect control where limited oracle headroom constrains selection gains.
\textbf{WNUT-17} \cite{derczynski2017wnut} (1{,}287 social media instances, 52\% zero F1) probes the signal-collapse boundary where model capability is insufficient.

\paragraph{Models and sampling.}
Both \textbf{Qwen3-8B} and \textbf{LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct} are fine-tuned with LoRA (rank$=$32, $\alpha{=}64$) for 5 epochs using LLaMA Factory \cite{zheng2024llamafactory} under identical training procedures, except Few-NERD models which use 3 epochs (early stopping at the best validation checkpoint; a 5-epoch control confirms LP selection remains positive though gains are halved; Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}).
Qwen3-8B's SciERC checkpoint has a minor train--test mismatch (empty thinking tokens); a quantitative analysis rules out this confound (Appendix~\ref{app:thinking_token}).
We draw $N{=}8$ samples at $T{=}1.0$ (primary), with $N{=}16$ for scaling experiments.\footnote{$N{=}16$ with 3 seeds verifies scaling and ranking stability ($\sigma{\leq}0.026$; Table~\ref{tab:multiseed}); gains beyond $N{=}8$ are sublinear (Appendix~\ref{app:n_scaling}).}
Inference uses vLLM with constrained decoding \cite{willard2023guided}.\footnote{Quality estimation uses a single primary seed (seed${=}42$); selection uses 2-seed averages for variance estimation. Three-seed experiments ($\sigma{\leq}0.026$; Table~\ref{tab:multiseed}) confirm ranking stability; the reduced seed count for primary results reflects computational budget.}

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Quality Estimation Results}
\label{sec:qe_results}

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{NER quality estimation ($\rho$ and AUROC; $N{=}8$, primary seed${=}42$; 3-seed stability $\sigma{\leq}0.026$ in Table~\ref{tab:multiseed}). Best in \textbf{bold}. RE results in Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}. $N{=}16$ in Table~\ref{tab:robustness}.}
\label{tab:quality_estimation}
\begin{tabular}{ll cc cc cc}
\toprule
& & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{SciERC}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{CoNLL (Qwen)}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{CoNLL (LLaMA)}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){3-4} \cmidrule(lr){5-6} \cmidrule(lr){7-8}
Signal & Family & $\rho$ & AUC & $\rho$ & AUC & $\rho$ & AUC \\
\midrule
VC & Structural & \textbf{.379} & \textbf{.663} & .424 & .789 & .426 & .709 \\
SJ  & Structural & .360 & .650 & .436 & .801 & \textbf{.431} & .704 \\
FK & Surface & .267 & .602 & .400 & .774 & .428 & .703 \\
EM  & Surface    & .295 & .614 & \textbf{.451} & \textbf{.816} & \textbf{.431} & .705 \\
\midrule
LP     & Model      & .205 & .578 & .316 & .753 & .307 & \textbf{.761} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

Table~\ref{tab:quality_estimation} presents $\rho$ and AUROC for five signals across tasks and models.\footnote{$\varepsilon{=}0.05$ applies only to selection (\S\ref{sec:selection_gap}); quality estimation is $\varepsilon$-free. Selection F1 is stable across $\varepsilon \in [0, 0.2]$ (Appendix~\ref{app:epsilon_robustness}).}

\paragraph{Signal rankings are difficulty-dependent.}
On SciERC (medium difficulty), structural signals lead (VC $\rho{=}0.379$, SJ $0.360$) over LP ($0.205$); bootstrap CIs confirm the gap ($\Delta\rho{=}{+}0.155$; Appendix~\ref{app:bootstrap_signal}).
On CoNLL (single-sample F1${\approx}$0.91), EM leads both $\rho$ and AUROC ($0.816$).
At the signal-collapse boundary (WNUT-17, macro F1${\approx}$42\%), full-set correlations are dominated by the zero/non-zero boundary (VC $\rho{=}0.724$ but conditional $\rho{=}0.038$; Table~\ref{tab:wnut}); LP shows the reverse (conditional $\rho{=}0.350$; Figure~\ref{fig:signal_heatmap}).

%% ============================================================
\subsection{The Correlation-Selection Gap}
\label{sec:selection_gap}

Two mechanisms block selection: instance degeneracy collapses the candidate pool for consensus-based approaches, and weak LP-quality alignment prevents exploiting the remaining headroom.
A methodological observation underlies both: four instance-level signals cannot rank candidates by construction; LP requires both low degeneracy and adequate alignment ($\rho \gtrsim 0.3$).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Best-of-$N$ NER selection (macro F1) on SciERC ($N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, 2-seed mean${\pm}\sigma$). LP is per-sample; others use consensus-proximity. Best non-oracle in \textbf{bold}. Non-tied subset in \S\ref{sec:discussion}; RE in Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}.}
\label{tab:selection}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Method & NER F1 & $p$ \\
\midrule
Oracle & $0.778 \pm 0.003$ & --- \\
Greedy ($T{=}0$) & $0.645 \pm 0.001$ & --- \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textit{Per-sample signal}} \\
LP           & $\mathbf{0.647} \pm 0.005$ & .29 \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textit{Instance-level signals (consensus-proximity selection)}} \\
SJ           & $0.646 \pm 0.002$ & .67 \\
FK           & $0.646 \pm 0.001$ & .79 \\
EM           & $0.637 \pm 0.001$ & --- \\
VC           & $0.623 \pm 0.004$ & --- \\
\midrule
Random           & $0.610 \pm 0.003$ & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Despite moderate quality estimation, no signal consistently improves selection over greedy (Table~\ref{tab:selection}).
Instance-level signals assign identical QE scores to all candidates; MBR conversion does not improve over greedy on the primary test bed.

\paragraph{No significant improvement over greedy.}
On SciERC ($n{=}529$, 2-seed sign tests\footnote{Seed variation $\sigma{\leq}0.026$ (Table~\ref{tab:multiseed}); MDE${\approx}$1.5~pp at 80\% power for $n{=}529$, so a 1~pp effect yields only ${\sim}$55\% power. Few-NERD ($n{=}32{,}565$; MDE${\approx}$0.22~pp) provides the definitive test.}), the oracle (0.778) reveals 13.3~pp of headroom, but LP achieves $+0.2$~pp ($p{=}0.29$), below the MDE (${\approx}$1.5~pp).
The pattern holds across CoNLL and WNUT-17 (Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}); under stringent TOST anchors, some configurations show significant \textit{degradation} (Appendix~\ref{app:tost}).
Few-NERD provides the definitive contrast: LP achieves $+1.36$~pp ($p{<}0.001$) at low degeneracy (\S\ref{sec:fewnerd}), while structural signals still degrade selection.
Instance-level signals predict \textit{which inputs} the model handles reliably, not \textit{which output} is best; closing this gap likely requires trained verifiers \cite{cobbe2021verifiers}.\footnote{The sole positive finding (non-tied LP subset, $+2.9$~pp, $p{=}0.004$; \S\ref{sec:discussion}) survives Bonferroni correction over 5 comparisons.}

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Cross-Dataset Contrast: Few-NERD}
\label{sec:fewnerd}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-dataset comparison of LP selection effectiveness (Qwen3-8B, $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$). LP selection gains are inversely associated with instance degeneracy across three datasets (full-set evaluation; see Table~\ref{tab:selection} footnote for gold-filtered protocol difference).\protect\footnotemark\protect\footnotemark}
\label{tab:fewnerd}
\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
\toprule
Dataset & $n$ & Greedy & Headroom & Degen\% & LP $\rho$ & LP $\Delta$ \\
\midrule
CoNLL & 2{,}756 & .908 & 4.0\,pp & 47.4\% & .316 & n.s. \\
SciERC & 551 & .614 & 13.8\,pp & 17.8\% & .205 & $+$0.42\,pp \\
Few-NERD & 32{,}565 & .748 & 12.4\,pp & 13.8\% & .421 & $\mathbf{+1.36}$\,pp \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}
\footnotetext{SciERC greedy (0.614) and headroom (13.8~pp) differ from Table~\ref{tab:selection} (gold-filtered $n{=}529$, greedy${=}0.645$) due to full-set evaluation; the ranking and significance are unchanged.}
\footnotetext{Instance degeneracy: fraction of gold-filtered instances where all $N$ samples produce identical instance-level F1.}

Few-NERD (Table~\ref{tab:fewnerd}) confirms that LP selection effectiveness is inversely associated with degeneracy.
Under standard early stopping, Few-NERD degeneracy is 13.8\% (training convergence modulates this rate, Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}), and LP achieves $+1.36$~pp (95\% CI $[+1.18, +1.53]$, $p{<}0.001$), while all structural signals degrade selection (VC $-1.79$, SJ $-2.14$~pp).

\paragraph{Two necessary conditions for LP selection.}
The cross-dataset gradient is consistent with an inverse relationship (47.4\% $\to$ n.s., 17.8\% $\to$ $+0.42$~pp, 13.8\% $\to$ $+1.36$~pp; $n{=}3$ datasets, exploratory given 5+ confounds; Figure~\ref{fig:degeneracy_scatter}), with supporting evidence from per-entity-type analysis within Few-NERD ($n{=}8$, $\rho{=}{-}0.881$, $p{=}0.004$; entity types share model, tokenizer, and temperature, so effective degrees of freedom may be lower than 6).
Low degeneracy alone is not sufficient: the LoRA-rank ablation (\S\ref{sec:robustness_main}) reduces degeneracy to 7.2\% yet LP does not improve selection because $\rho(\text{LP}, \text{F1}){\approx}0.24$.
Few-NERD satisfies both conditions: low degeneracy (13.8\%) and strong alignment ($\rho{\approx}0.5$; per-type detail in Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_lp}).
All five signals achieve positive QE on Few-NERD (LP $\rho{=}0.42$, VC $0.39$), yet only LP translates QE into selection; the gap confirms that strong instance-level QE cannot rank candidates within an instance.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_degeneracy_lp_scatter.pdf}
\caption{Instance degeneracy vs.\ LP selection gain. Blue circles: cross-dataset ($n{=}3$, exploratory). Orange triangles: per-entity-type within Few-NERD ($n{=}8$, $\rho{=}{-}0.881$, $p{=}0.004$; types share model and tokenizer). The dashed line at ${\sim}$15\%\protect\footnotemark{} separates regimes where LP improves (left) vs.\ shows insufficient evidence (right).}
\label{fig:degeneracy_scatter}
\end{figure}

\footnotetext{Boundary between Person (13.4\%, $+1.25$~pp) and Art (19.8\%, $-0.17$~pp).}

\paragraph{LP selection scales with sample budget.}
Doubling $N$ from 8 to 16 on Few-NERD yields $+0.52$~pp (paired $p{=}0.003$, CI $[+0.17, +0.87]$), driven by reduced degeneracy (14.6\% $\to$ 7.0\%) expanding headroom (12.1 $\to$ 15.4~pp), while LP ranking quality remains stable ($\rho{\approx}0.62$).
Structural signals remain uniformly negative at $N{=}16$; the gap persists regardless of sample budget.

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Robustness}
\label{sec:robustness_main}

Consistency signals are robust across $T{\in}\{0.7, 1.0\}$ with sublinear $N$-scaling gains (Table~\ref{tab:robustness}); LP shows the largest temperature sensitivity ($\Delta\rho{=}{+}0.044$).
Signal rankings are stable across seeds (Kendall's $W{=}1.0$ at $N{=}8$, though statistical precision is limited with $m{=}3$ raters, exact $p{\approx}0.028$; Table~\ref{tab:ranking-stability}).
NER calibration is reasonable (ECE 0.069--0.096; Appendix~\ref{app:calibration}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Scale ablation: Qwen3-8B vs.\ 4B NER quality estimation ($\rho$; $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, seed${=}42$). 8B values identical to Table~\ref{tab:quality_estimation}; 4B uses matched evaluation protocol. RE scale ablation in Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}.}
\label{tab:scale_ablation}

